WEBVTT

00:00.980 --> 00:02.915
Imagine you run a momentum book.

00:02.955 --> 00:04.352
You buy recent winners,

00:04.392 --> 00:05.765
sell recent losers,

00:05.805 --> 00:06.952
and hedge the common risk

00:06.992 --> 00:09.300
your factor model describes.

00:09.340 --> 00:10.525
You want to keep one thing:

00:10.565 --> 00:12.435
the momentum tilt.

00:12.475 --> 00:14.822
And momentum is not a quiet bet.

00:14.862 --> 00:16.760
In the summer of 2026,

00:16.800 --> 00:22.060
MSCI's index of AI hardware stocks fell about 20% in five weeks,

00:22.100 --> 00:26.620
and MSCI found that momentum split the winners from the losers.

00:26.660 --> 00:29.045
No hedge that keeps the tilt stops that.

00:29.085 --> 00:31.970
But you should know how much of your momentum your model sees,

00:32.010 --> 00:33.815
and what it leaves in your book.

00:34.295 --> 00:37.180
Now your risk team upgrades the factor model.

00:37.220 --> 00:39.067
Same six standard factors,

00:39.107 --> 00:41.080
still three statistical ones,

00:41.120 --> 00:45.030
but their loadings are now built from 153 characteristics,

00:45.070 --> 00:46.305
not 36,

00:46.345 --> 00:50.140
and the model fits the returns it was estimated on better.

00:50.180 --> 00:52.190
When you hedge the new model's loadings,

00:52.230 --> 00:54.140
how much of your momentum goes with them,

00:54.180 --> 00:56.125
and what book is left?

00:56.165 --> 00:57.025
Short answer:

00:57.065 --> 00:59.025
the new model sees less of your momentum,

00:59.065 --> 01:01.062
so the hedge leaves you a different book.

01:01.102 --> 01:02.395
Here is why.

01:06.075 --> 01:07.285
Start with the model:

01:07.325 --> 01:08.710
six familiar factors,

01:08.750 --> 01:11.035
like the market, value and momentum,

01:11.075 --> 01:14.120
and three statistical ones learned from the data.

01:14.160 --> 01:15.732
A stock's loading on each factor

01:15.772 --> 01:17.145
comes from a recipe:

01:17.185 --> 01:19.220
weights on the stock's characteristics,

01:19.260 --> 01:21.607
each ranked across stocks every month.

01:21.647 --> 01:24.430
The weights are re-estimated once a year.

01:24.470 --> 01:25.330
The idea:

01:25.370 --> 01:27.830
a stock's loadings move as the firm changes,

01:27.870 --> 01:30.240
because its characteristics do.

01:30.280 --> 01:32.040
Apply a recipe to every stock,

01:32.080 --> 01:33.140
and you get a list:

01:33.180 --> 01:35.150
one loading per stock.

01:35.190 --> 01:37.687
Even the momentum factor works this way.

01:37.727 --> 01:40.925
A stock's loading on it comes from an estimated recipe

01:40.965 --> 01:42.987
that can use many characteristics,

01:43.027 --> 01:45.460
not simply its momentum rank.

01:45.500 --> 01:47.260
So the model's momentum loadings

01:47.300 --> 01:49.205
are not your momentum book.

01:49.785 --> 01:51.957
Your momentum book is a recipe too:

01:51.997 --> 01:54.095
all of its weight on one characteristic,

01:54.135 --> 01:56.780
and again, one number per stock.

01:56.820 --> 01:59.017
So hedging asks a simple question:

01:59.057 --> 02:00.517
how much does your list overlap

02:00.557 --> 02:02.590
with the model's nine lists?

02:02.630 --> 02:05.640
The tempting shortcut is to work with the recipes alone:

02:05.680 --> 02:08.127
check, or hedge, the weights on paper.

02:08.167 --> 02:10.400
That is comparing on paper.

02:10.440 --> 02:12.037
Here is the simplest case.

02:12.077 --> 02:13.737
Book-to-market and earnings-to-price

02:13.777 --> 02:15.350
share no ingredients.

02:15.390 --> 02:18.210
On paper, their overlap is zero.

02:18.250 --> 02:20.847
But they can rank many of the same firms as cheap.

02:20.887 --> 02:22.547
So apply both to the stocks,

02:22.587 --> 02:24.160
multiply stock by stock,

02:24.200 --> 02:25.645
and average.

02:25.685 --> 02:27.707
In this made-up five-stock example,

02:27.747 --> 02:29.720
scaled by each list's own size,

02:29.760 --> 02:32.230
the two lists are 0.8 aligned.

02:32.270 --> 02:33.492
That is the ruler:

02:33.532 --> 02:36.205
it measures recipes by the stocks they point to,

02:36.245 --> 02:38.765
not by their ingredients on paper.

02:38.805 --> 02:40.090
Nothing exotic.

02:40.130 --> 02:44.140
Scaled, it works like a correlation of the two lists across the stocks.

02:44.180 --> 02:46.840
The model already uses it when it is estimated.

02:46.880 --> 02:48.015
The slip comes later,

02:48.055 --> 02:50.675
when its loadings are read and hedged.

02:50.715 --> 02:52.262
What is new is what it reveals

02:52.302 --> 02:53.687
when the model changes,

02:53.727 --> 02:55.860
and as the year goes on.

02:55.900 --> 02:58.072
Because it is built from the stocks at hand.

02:58.112 --> 02:59.960
Next month the ranks reshuffle.

03:00.000 --> 03:01.135
Same recipes,

03:01.175 --> 03:03.645
and the alignment is 0.7.

03:03.685 --> 03:05.745
Coverage is the share of your momentum list

03:05.785 --> 03:08.170
that the model's nine lists can rebuild.

03:08.210 --> 03:08.870
Roughly,

03:08.910 --> 03:11.145
that is what a loading hedge would strip out.

03:11.185 --> 03:12.520
It describes stocks,

03:12.560 --> 03:15.080
not how much risk is removed.

03:15.120 --> 03:17.067
The shortcut shows up in the book.

03:17.107 --> 03:20.280
Hedge a momentum book by comparing recipes on paper,

03:20.320 --> 03:23.080
and on average more than half of the hedged book's squared weight

03:23.120 --> 03:25.292
still sits on the model's loadings.

03:25.332 --> 03:26.455
Hedge with the ruler,

03:26.495 --> 03:27.825
and none does.

03:28.505 --> 03:29.752
Now the three checks.

03:29.792 --> 03:33.200
First: how much of your momentum does the model see?

03:33.240 --> 03:35.525
With 153 characteristics,

03:35.565 --> 03:37.760
in-sample fit improves.

03:37.800 --> 03:40.147
But average coverage of a momentum position

03:40.187 --> 03:43.420
falls from 82% to 62%.

03:43.460 --> 03:45.470
Book-to-market and size fall too,

03:45.510 --> 03:48.855
in each of 19 annual estimation windows.

03:48.895 --> 03:49.630
Better fit,

03:49.670 --> 03:51.750
but less of your momentum in view.

03:52.430 --> 03:54.475
Second: what book is left?

03:54.515 --> 03:56.800
The hedge removes what the model sees.

03:56.840 --> 03:59.487
What is left is the momentum the model does not see,

03:59.527 --> 04:01.985
scaled back up to the same tilt.

04:02.025 --> 04:03.810
Less shared means less stripped out,

04:03.850 --> 04:05.135
and, other things equal,

04:05.175 --> 04:07.020
less to scale back up.

04:07.060 --> 04:10.720
With 36 characteristics, the hedged book averages positions

04:10.760 --> 04:13.205
of 2.3 times capital.

04:13.245 --> 04:14.880
With 153,

04:14.920 --> 04:16.780
it averages 1.6,

04:16.820 --> 04:20.890
and monthly turnover falls from 81% to 58%.

04:20.930 --> 04:22.177
Same momentum target.

04:22.217 --> 04:23.925
Two different books.

04:23.965 --> 04:26.425
Smaller is not automatically safer:

04:26.465 --> 04:28.337
the risk difference between these two books

04:28.377 --> 04:30.420
is not statistically clear.

04:31.075 --> 04:33.395
Third: is the ruler up to date?

04:33.435 --> 04:35.770
The model is re-estimated each March.

04:35.810 --> 04:37.345
But characteristics change,

04:37.385 --> 04:40.105
and stocks enter and leave every month.

04:40.145 --> 04:42.180
Measured on last March's stocks,

04:42.220 --> 04:44.005
the hedge stops being fully neutral

04:44.045 --> 04:45.380
to the model's own loadings

04:45.420 --> 04:46.817
as the year goes on.

04:46.857 --> 04:48.330
That sounds obvious.

04:48.370 --> 04:50.840
The question is how much it matters.

04:50.880 --> 04:53.040
Measured on current stocks instead,

04:53.080 --> 04:55.615
the momentum hedge has an estimated 40%

04:55.655 --> 04:58.025
lower subsequent variance.

04:58.065 --> 05:00.600
Across twelve exploratory comparisons,

05:00.640 --> 05:04.150
the estimates are 27 to 69% lower.

05:04.190 --> 05:05.875
Mean return and turnover

05:05.915 --> 05:08.170
do not improve across the board.

05:08.850 --> 05:11.060
The same overlap moves credit.

05:11.100 --> 05:12.510
When two lists overlap,

05:12.550 --> 05:15.610
the shared part can be credited to either set of factors:

05:15.650 --> 05:18.720
the standard ones or the statistical ones.

05:18.760 --> 05:20.970
In the 36-characteristic model,

05:21.010 --> 05:24.895
the return credited to the six standard factors for a size position

05:24.935 --> 05:28.395
can rise from about one basis point a month to 46,

05:28.435 --> 05:31.740
while the combined factor return stays the same.

05:32.495 --> 05:34.490
Back to your momentum book.

05:34.530 --> 05:36.665
The ruler cannot predict an unwind,

05:36.705 --> 05:39.875
and a hedge that keeps the tilt still takes the hit.

05:39.915 --> 05:41.425
What it tells you, beforehand,

05:41.465 --> 05:44.400
is how much of your momentum each model's loadings share,

05:44.440 --> 05:45.725
what the hedge strips out,

05:45.765 --> 05:47.470
and what book is left.

05:48.250 --> 05:49.072
So don't only ask

05:49.112 --> 05:51.470
what a factor model explains.

05:51.510 --> 05:52.770
Ask what it makes you hedge,

05:52.810 --> 05:54.465
and what it makes you hold.
